<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Zemurray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Zemurray (1877–1961) was an American businessman, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisinau">Kishinev</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, who built a banana empire in Honduras. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyamel-fruit-company">Cuyamel Fruit Company</a> backed the invasion of 1910–1911 that restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-bonilla">Manuel Bonilla</a> to power, winning large concessions. In 1929 he sold Cuyamel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>, becoming its largest shareholder, and he ran United Fruit from 1933 until 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-zemurray.jpg" alt="Samuel Zemurray" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Los Angeles Times · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sam_Zemurray,_1934.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
